> Pardon my question, but are there any proper studies that show that
> any of this stuff has a real effect on performance?

> Perry

Firstly, here's some studies just relating to brainwave control:

At the University of Colorado Medical Centre and at the Biofeedback Centre
in Denver, Dr. Thomas Budzyski found that when people were trained to
achieve and maintain theta brain waves using biofeedback techniques, they
learnt things "much faster". And many emotional and attitudinal problems
were solved at the same time. (Biofeedback and the twilight state of 
consciousness. Consciousness and Self Regulation Vol. 1 Advances in Research. 
G.E. Schwartz and D. Shapiro eds   New York Pieum Press   1976) 

Doctors Elmer and Alyce Green, pioneers of biofeedback research at the
Menninger Clinic in Topeka, Kansas, trained subjects to control almost all
the "involuntary" responses - heart rate, muscle tension, sweat gland
activity etc. This research team were able "recently" [the book I'm taking
this from was first published in 1985 - sjw] to correlate an increased level
of Alpha & Theta brainwaves with a substantial acceleration in the 
acquisition of new knowledge.

Secondly, there are numerous studies with positive findings on the
'Accelerated Learning' techniques originally developed by Dr. Georgi Lozanov
of Bulgaria. Part of the techniques involve playing Baroque music in the
background. The music was found to increase alpha and theta brainwaves at
the expense of beta waves. I think the idea is that as the excess of beta
waves in the left hemisphere is diminished this allows a more balanced
performance with each hemisphere allowed to play its part, combining
synthesis and analysis. Dr. Bernard Glueck at the Institute for Living in
Hartford, Connecticut, found that meditators showed an increased synchronicity
between the left and right hemispheres. He suggested that this showed an
improved communication through the corpus callosum, achieved by relaxation
and increased alpha waves.

But in these studies there could well have been other factors involved, e.g.
games to review the previous day's learning, creating a 'mental map of the
information due to be learned'. And the accent was on learning languages.
 
I'm keeping an open mind on whether these methods work, but if we can come
up with low cost, practical methods that Extropians can use to check such
things out for ourselves, it's worth doing our own unofficial studies. You
could possibly get enough stereo separation by just using speaker output from
a computer and keyboard organ, positioned a few feet either side, or just use
one earpiece from the organ, eliminating the need for any wiring. 

